# Typing Component Props in TypeScript

There are two main approaches to type component props in TypeScript:

## 1. **Inline Type Annotation** (Simple cases)
For components with few props, you can type them directly in the function parameters:

```tsx
function MyButton({ title }: { title: string }) {
  return (
    <button>{title}</button>
  );
}
```

## 2. **Interface or Type Definition** (Recommended)
For more complex props, define an `interface` or `type` to describe your props:

```tsx
interface MyButtonProps {
  /** The text to display inside the button */
  title: string;
  /** Whether the button can be interacted with */
  disabled?: boolean;
}

function MyButton({ title, disabled }: MyButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button disabled={disabled}>{title}</button>
  );
}
```

## Key Points:
- Props should be described as an **object type**
- Use either `type` or `interface` (both work well)
- For optional properties, use the `?` modifier (e.g., `disabled?: boolean`)
- Use `React.ReactNode` for props that accept React elements:

```tsx
interface MyButtonProps {
  color: string;
  children?: React.ReactNode;
}
```

The `interface`/`type` approach is preferred once you have more than a few fields, as it keeps your code more organized and readable.

